    "blocks": [
      {
        "rect": [
          99.0,
          88.74471282958984,
          1811.36767578125,
          175.87991333007812
        ],
        "text": "Overfitting: “Correct” specifications aren’t enough?\n"
      },
      {
        "rect": [
          99.0,
          332.3983154296875,
          1118.10888671875,
          388.7992248535156
        ],
        "text": "• How do people really create and use reward functions? \n"
      },
      {
        "rect": [
          147.0,
          410.80853271484375,
          1269.5863037109375,
          467.2094421386719
        ],
        "text": "• Often iteratively while observing behaviors that they generate \n"
      },
      {
        "rect": [
          147.0,
          489.21868896484375,
          1193.10986328125,
          545.61962890625
        ],
        "text": "• Can overfit to particular algorithms and hyperparameters \n"
      },
      {
        "rect": [
          147.0,
          567.62890625,
          1177.6807861328125,
          624.0298461914062
        ],
        "text": "• Won’t generalize to new algorithms / parameter settings \n"
      },
      {
        "rect": [
          99.0,
          724.4493408203125,
          1257.522216796875,
          780.8502807617188
        ],
        "text": "• What if state distribution changes from training to deployment? \n"
      },
      {
        "rect": [
          147.0,
          802.8594970703125,
          1788.587646484375,
          859.2604370117188
        ],
        "text": "• Policy may have overfit to a feature that was perfectly correlated with good performance in \n"
      },
      {
        "rect": [
          186.36000061035156,
          849.54296875,
          967.222900390625,
          895.3973388671875
        ],
        "text": "training distribution, but not test distribution \n"
      },
      {
        "rect": [
          147.0,
          922.780029296875,
          1819.3677978515625,
          979.1809692382812
        ],
        "text": "• May cause it to do dangerous things in test distribution when correlation is not longer perfect\n"
      }
    ]
  }
}
